---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Heatmap from '../components/Heatmap.astro';
import Icons from '../components/Icons.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const projects = (await getCollection('projects')).filter((p) => p.id !== 'keysreallysafe').sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const posts = (await getCollection('writing')).sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());
const fmt = (d) => d.toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'short', day: 'numeric' });
---
<Base home={true}>
  <main>
    <div class="hero">
      <h1>George Shostakovych</h1>
      <div class="sub">I build the guardrails that let coding agents work unattended.</div>
      <p>Solo founder in the Bay Area. Since October I have been building for coding agents that run with nobody watching: <a href="/projects/keysreallysafe/">Keysrs</a>, so an agent gets a short-lived token instead of the API key; <a href="/projects/brakeline/">brakeline</a>, so a stuck agent stops before it burns the budget or the work; and <a href="/projects/lie-you-die/">lie-you-die</a>, a benchmark that checks what an agent did against what it reported. Before that I took NodFirst, an eldercare app, from a blank repo to the App Store in five weeks.</p>
      <Icons />
    </div>

    <section id="work">
      <h2>Work</h2>
      <div class="body">
      <div class="featured">
        <h3>Keysrs</h3>
        <p>An API-key vault for coding agents on a Mac. Keys stay in the Keychain behind Touch ID. Agents ask over MCP and get a token scoped to one key, one host and a time limit; cloud VMs and sandboxes reach the same gateway over SSH or a relay in your own Cloudflare account, and the real key is swapped in on the Mac. Free, open source, MIT.</p>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat"><b>22</b><span>releases since Sep 4</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><b>5</b><span>agent clients set up over MCP</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><b>0.15</b><span>latest release, Nov 3</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip">Swift</span><span class="chip">Keychain</span><span class="chip">Touch ID</span><span class="chip">MCP</span><span class="chip">Cloudflare Workers</span><span class="chip">Homebrew</span>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem"><a class="more" href="/projects/keysreallysafe/">How it works and what changed</a> · <a class="more" href="https://keysrs.com">keysrs.com</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid2">
      {projects.map((p) => (
        <a class="pcard" href={`/projects/${p.id}/`}>
          <div class="when">{p.data.period}</div>
          <h3>{p.data.title} <span class="arr">→</span></h3>
          <p>{p.data.summary}</p>
          <div class="chips">{p.data.stack?.map((s) => <span class="chip">{s}</span>)}</div>
        </a>
      ))}
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:2rem"><Heatmap /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="system">
      <h2>The system<small>Since October: what I built so coding agents can work with nobody watching, and what each step taught me. The NodFirst build, week by week, is <a href="/projects/nodfirst/">on its own page</a>.</small></h2>
      <div class="body">
      <ol class="timeline">
        <li><div class="when">Oct 10</div><div><h3>A seatbelt for unattended runs</h3><p><a href="/projects/brakeline/">brakeline</a>: one Rust binary on every tool call that stops loops, caps spend, refuses catastrophic commands and snapshots the work before destructive ones. A cloned repo can tighten its limits but never loosen them. <span class="lesson">Lesson: built-in undo does not cover the shell. Snapshot before the command, not after.</span></p></div></li>
        <li><div class="when">Oct 14 to 15</div><div><h3>What survives compaction</h3><p><a href="/projects/eve-tokyo/">eve-tokyo</a> replays long sessions through eve's real compaction engine. When it kept only the start of a tool result, summaries called a failing build passing in 72 of 180 checkpoints. I reported two bugs; the eve team fixed both the next day. <span class="lesson">Lesson: keep the end of a tool's output. That is where the failure is.</span></p></div></li>
        <li><div class="when">Oct 15 to 16</div><div><h3>Do agents admit their shortcuts?</h3><p><a href="/projects/lie-you-die/">lie-you-die</a> gives agents tasks they cannot finish honestly. Over 256 runs one model never cheated; two took shortcuts in up to half their runs and usually reported them as fixes. Raising the stakes made them cheat less. <span class="lesson">Lesson: grade an agent by its diff and its command log, never by its report.</span></p></div></li>
        <li><div class="when">Oct 21 to 27</div><div><h3>Keys stay home, agents travel</h3><p>Keysrs 0.12 and 0.13: VMs and sandboxes get tokens, not keys, and their calls come back to the Mac over SSH or a relay in my own Cloudflare account. Grant profiles, renewal in place, and a relay that only talks to paired agents.</p></div></li>
        <li><div class="when">Oct 26 to 27</div><div><h3>Long runs find different bugs</h3><p>Multi-hour eve sessions on real coding tasks. A 429 on the compaction call ended the whole session instead of one turn; the eve team fixed it within a day, and the 0.84.1 release notes credit the report. The same week I reported a step that stays frozen for 14 minutes after a crash. <span class="lesson">Lesson: a benchmark that runs for minutes misses what breaks in hours.</span></p></div></li>
        <li><div class="when">Oct 29 to 31</div><div><h3>Let the agent ask</h3><p>Keysrs 0.14 is an MCP server: the agent scans a project, wraps its keys and asks for a grant, and I approve with Touch ID. In the hands-on test the agent read a provider's 401 as the gateway refusing it, so every response now says which side wrote it. <span class="lesson">Lesson: an agent explains an error with the nearest story. Make every error name its source.</span></p></div></li>
        <li><div class="when">Oct 31 to Nov 4</div><div><h3>Upstream</h3><p>A fix PR on <a href="https://github.com/Infisical/infisical/pull/9073">Infisical</a> for concurrent creates that returned 500 instead of "already exists", and two more eve reports, one with a fix now in review. <a href="/projects/upstream/">All of them</a>. <span class="lesson">Lesson: a report comes with a repo the maintainer can run in minutes.</span></p></div></li>
        <li><div class="when">Nov 3</div><div><h3>Cut what nobody looks at</h3><p>Keysrs 0.15 removed the usage meter it used to open with, and the opt-in analytics. Once the app ran headless the meter had no screen worth keeping and still cost a background poll; the analytics had no users. <span class="lesson">Lesson: a feature that works but nobody sees is still a cost.</span></p></div></li>
      </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="writing">
      <h2>Writing<small>Notes on shipping alone, and on what models actually do.</small></h2>
      <div class="body">
      {posts.map((w) => (
        <a class="row" href={`/writing/${w.id}/`}>
          <div class="when">{fmt(w.data.date)}</div>
          <div><h3>{w.data.title} <span class="arr">→</span></h3><p>{w.data.summary}</p></div>
        </a>
      ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="about">
      <h2>About</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
      <p>I am a founder in the San Francisco Bay Area. What I am good at is not a language or a framework. It is building the system that turns an idea into a shipped product when there is one person to do it: the agents, the specs, the review gates, the deploy path, the guardrails, and the habit of writing down what broke.</p>
      <p>The agents typed most of the code on this site's projects. I built the thing that made them useful, and the timeline above, with <a href="/projects/nodfirst/">the NodFirst build log</a>, is the most honest CV I have.</p>
      <p>Before NodFirst I built a distressed-credit screener that a fund uses daily. I set NodFirst down in September, picked it back up in October, and paused it again when no new calls came. Since then I have been building for coding agents that work unattended, and measuring what they actually do.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="contact">
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
      <p>Email is best: <a href="mailto:georgeshostakovich@gmail.com">georgeshostakovich@gmail.com</a>. I answer investors, founders building with agents, and anyone measuring what models and agents actually do.</p>
      <Icons />
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <script>
    const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.nav li a')];
    const secs = links.map((a) => document.querySelector(a.getAttribute('href')));
    const io = new IntersectionObserver((es) => {
      es.forEach((e) => { if (e.isIntersecting) links.forEach((l) => l.classList.toggle('on', l.getAttribute('href') === '#' + e.target.id)); });
    }, { rootMargin: '-30% 0px -60% 0px' });
    secs.forEach((s) => s && io.observe(s));
    links.forEach((l) => l.addEventListener('click', () => document.getElementById('nav').classList.remove('open')));
  </script>
</Base>
